WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.980
Hashing and encryption are often confused, but they solve different problems.

2
00:00:05.080 --> 00:00:08.520
Encryption is designed to be reversible with the right key.

3
00:00:08.620 --> 00:00:12.860
Password hashing for storage is designed for one-way verification.

4
00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:21.200
During login, the application processes the submitted password with the stored parameters and compares the result to the stored verifier.

5
00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:26.820
It should not decrypt or recover the original password because recovery should not be possible.

6
00:00:27.100 --> 00:00:31.420
A salt is a unique value used with each password before hashing.

7
00:00:31.520 --> 00:00:39.360
When used correctly, per-password salts help ensure that two users with the same password do not have the same stored verifier.

8
00:00:39.460 --> 00:00:43.140
Salts also make broad precomputed comparisons less useful.

9
00:00:43.240 --> 00:00:49.320
Salts are normally stored with the password hash because their role is uniqueness, not secrecy.

10
00:00:49.420 --> 00:00:56.780
A missing or reused salt weakens the design because repeated passwords become easier to recognize.

11
00:00:57.060 --> 00:01:04.420
Work factors and computational cost are what make password hashing intentionally expensive.

12
00:01:04.520 --> 00:01:17.560
A good password hashing design should be slow enough to make large-scale offline guessing difficult if hashes are exposed, but not so slow that normal login, account creation, and reset flows become unreliable.

13
00:01:17.660 --> 00:01:25.420
Memory-hard hashing concepts add memory cost as part of the defensive design, making some large-scale guessing strategies harder to scale.

14
00:01:25.700 --> 00:01:34.020
Fast general-purpose hashes are not appropriate for password storage because speed helps attackers test guesses quickly after a hash database is exposed.

15
00:01:34.120 --> 00:01:40.840
Password storage should use vetted password hashing algorithms through mature libraries or framework-supported patterns.

16
00:01:40.940 --> 00:01:49.820
Custom implementations are risky because small mistakes in salts, parameters, comparisons, encoding, or upgrades can undermine the entire control.
